content.text:
Yes. You need one `Error` class at minimum, because a thrown value needs a stack trace and the handler needs an `instanceof` check, but the three subclasses are optional.

## The options

| Option | How a service fails | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| A. Subclasses (current) | `throw new NotFoundError('…')` | Four classes. Nothing ties the status 404 to the code `NOT_FOUND` except each constructor. |
| B. One class and a status map | `throw notFound('…')` | One class, one map, three small functions. `CLAUDE.md` needs a two-line change. |
| C. Result values, no throw | `return { ok: false, error: … }` | Each caller must check the result. A `better-sqlite3` transaction rolls back only on a throw, so a returned error inside one would commit. |
| D. `@fastify/error` | `throw new NotFoundError()` made by `createError` | A new dependency, and it still makes classes. |

## Recommendation: option B

It is the smallest, and the status comes from the code in one place:

```ts
import { errorCodes, type ErrorCode } from '@venue/contract';

const STATUS_BY_CODE = {
  [errorCodes.VALIDATION_ERROR]: 400,
  [errorCodes.NOT_FOUND]: 404,
  [errorCodes.CONFLICT]: 409,
} as const;

type AppErrorCode = keyof typeof STATUS_BY_CODE;

export class AppError extends Error {
  readonly statusCode: number;

  constructor(readonly code: AppErrorCode, message: string, readonly details?: unknown) {
    super(message);
    this.statusCode = STATUS_BY_CODE[code];
  }
}

export const validationFailed = (message: string, details?: unknown) =>
  new AppError(errorCodes.VALIDATION_ERROR, message, details);
export const notFound = (message: string) => new AppError(errorCodes.NOT_FOUND, message);
export const conflict = (message: string) => new AppError(errorCodes.CONFLICT, message);
```

The error handler does not change, because it already checks only `instanceof AppError`.

Stay with option A if you want the brief's phrase "typed errors" to map to named types a reviewer can see at a glance. I would not take option C: the audit trail and the booking check both run inside synchronous transactions, and those need a throw to roll back.

If you choose B, three places change: `errors.ts`, the three `throw` lines in `error-handler.test.ts`, and the Errors section of `CLAUDE.md`, which names `ValidationError`, `NotFoundError` and `ConflictError`. Say the word and I make the change.

